---
layout: base.njk
title: Compare
permalink: /compare/
description: How Atomdrift Scan compares to Socket, Aikido, VirusTotal, ClamAV and the rest on live zero-day malware — measured daily, every sample published.
---

<style>
  .compare { max-width: 1000px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 4px 24px 64px; }

  /* one bar palette for every engine: hostile solid, suspicious pale, grey = unreadable.
     Atomdrift is marked by its label, not by a colour of its own. */
  :root {
    --h: #9abe25; --s: #cfe09a; --na: #dfe3e8;
    --t-hostile: #dc2626; --t-suspicious: #e0a526; --t-benign: #2f9e63; --t-nodata: #7b8494;
  }

  /* ---------- hero ---------- */
  .cmp-hero { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.45fr 1fr; gap: 28px; align-items: start; padding: 18px 0 4px; }
  .cmp-hero .kick { font-family: 'Oxanium', sans-serif; font-size: .66rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: .15em;
                    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--slate); margin-bottom: 9px; }
  /* No hard break: a <br> splits at the same word whatever the column width, which
     is why it read as arbitrary. balance lets the browser even out the lines itself. */
  .cmp-hero h1 { font-family: 'Oxanium', sans-serif; font-size: 2.15rem; font-weight: 800; color: var(--carbon);
                 letter-spacing: -1.1px; line-height: 1.03; margin: 0; text-wrap: balance; }
  .cmp-hero h1 em { font-style: normal; color: var(--atomdrift-blue); }
  .cmp-hero .say { margin: 12px 0 0; color: var(--iron); line-height: 1.6; font-size: .95rem; }
  .cmp-hero .say b { color: var(--carbon); font-weight: 600; }
  .replaces { margin: 12px 0 0; font-size: .8rem; color: var(--slate); line-height: 1.5; }
  .replaces b { color: var(--graphite); font-weight: 600; }
  .cta-row { display: flex; gap: 9px; flex-wrap: wrap; margin: 15px 0 0; }
  .cta { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; font-family: 'Oxanium', sans-serif; font-weight: 700;
         font-size: .82rem; padding: 8px 15px; border-radius: 7px; text-decoration: none;
         border: 1.5px solid var(--border); color: var(--carbon); transition: border-color .15s, color .15s, background .15s; }
  .cta:hover { border-color: var(--atomdrift-blue); color: var(--atomdrift-blue); }
  .cta-primary { background: var(--toxic); border-color: var(--toxic); color: var(--carbon); font-weight: 800; }
  .cta-primary:hover { background: var(--toxic-dark); border-color: var(--toxic-dark); color: #fff; }
  .instl { margin-top: 11px; font-family: 'SF Mono', Consolas, monospace; font-size: .78rem; background: var(--mist);
           border-radius: 6px; padding: 8px 12px; color: var(--carbon); overflow-x: auto; }
  .badges { display: flex; gap: 6px; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-top: 12px; }
  .bdg { font-family: 'Oxanium', sans-serif; font-size: .62rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: .05em;
         text-transform: uppercase; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 5px; padding: 3px 8px;
         color: var(--iron); white-space: nowrap; }  /* or 'illumos' orphans onto its own line */
  .bdg.hot { border-color: var(--toxic-dark); color: var(--toxic-dark); background: var(--toxic-dim); }

  /* Our own two numbers, not a face-off. The comparison lives in the leaderboard
     directly below; repeating it here cost width the headline column needed. */
  .proof { background: var(--carbon); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px 20px; color: #fff;
           display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px; }
  .proof .scores { display: flex; gap: 26px; }
  .proof .n { font-family: 'Oxanium', sans-serif; font-size: 2.7rem; font-weight: 800; line-height: .85;
              letter-spacing: -2px; color: var(--toxic); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .proof .n.fp { color: #fff; }
  .proof .l { font-size: .6rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: .09em; color: #b9bfc9; margin-top: 8px;
              text-transform: uppercase; font-family: 'Oxanium', sans-serif; }
  .proof .at { font-family: 'SF Mono', Consolas, monospace; font-size: .72rem; color: #d5d9d2;
               border-top: 1px solid #2a2e28; padding-top: 11px; }
  .proof .at b { color: var(--toxic); font-weight: 700; }
  .proof .dial { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 7px; }
  .proof input[type=range] { -webkit-appearance: none; appearance: none; width: 100%; background: transparent;
                             margin: 0; cursor: ew-resize; }
  .proof input[type=range]::-webkit-slider-runnable-track { height: 3px; border-radius: 2px; background: #33382f; }
  .proof input[type=range]::-moz-range-track { height: 3px; border-radius: 2px; background: #33382f; }
  .proof input[type=range]::-webkit-slider-thumb { -webkit-appearance: none; width: 13px; height: 13px;
    border-radius: 50%; background: var(--toxic); margin-top: -5px; transition: transform .12s ease; }
  .proof input[type=range]::-moz-range-thumb { width: 13px; height: 13px; border: 0; border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--toxic); transition: transform .12s ease; }
  .proof input[type=range]:hover::-webkit-slider-thumb { transform: scale(1.25); }
  .proof input[type=range]:hover::-moz-range-thumb { transform: scale(1.25); }
  .proof input[type=range]:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--toxic); outline-offset: 5px; border-radius: 4px; }
  .proof .ends { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; font-family: 'SF Mono', Consolas, monospace;
                 font-size: .58rem; color: #71787f; }
  .proof p { margin: 0; font-size: .74rem; line-height: 1.55; color: #b9bfc9; }
  .proof p b { color: #eef0f3; font-weight: 600; }

  /* ---------- sections ---------- */
  .compare h2 { font-family: 'Oxanium', sans-serif; font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 800; color: var(--carbon);
                border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 40px 0 4px; letter-spacing: -.3px;
                display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 9px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .compare h2 .note { font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; font-size: .72rem; font-weight: 400; color: var(--slate); }
  /* One measure for every paragraph, expressed in rem rather than ch: these blocks
     run at different font sizes, so a shared ch value gives them different physical
     widths and the column edge goes ragged where they stack. */
  .compare .sub, .compare .fig-note, .cmp-hero .say, .replaces { max-width: 34rem; }
  .compare .sub { color: var(--iron); font-size: .88rem; margin: 10px 0 14px; }
  .compare .sub b { color: var(--carbon); font-weight: 600; }

  /* ---------- bars ---------- */
  .rows { display: grid; grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr max-content; gap: 6px 12px; align-items: center; margin: 12px 0 8px; }
  .rows .rn { font-family: 'Oxanium', sans-serif; font-weight: 600; font-size: .8rem; white-space: nowrap; color: var(--graphite); }
  .rows .rn.us { color: var(--atomdrift-blue); font-weight: 800; }
  .rows .tr { height: 17px; background: var(--mist); border-radius: 4px; overflow: hidden; display: flex;
              box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px rgba(0,0,0,.05); }
  .rows .hb { height: 100%; background: var(--h); }
  .rows .sb { height: 100%; background: var(--s); }
  .rows .un { height: 100%; background: var(--na); margin-left: auto; }
  .rows .rv { font-family: 'Oxanium', sans-serif; font-weight: 800; font-size: .8rem; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
              text-align: right; min-width: 36px; color: var(--carbon); }
  .legend { display: flex; gap: 8px 16px; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; font-size: .72rem; color: var(--iron); }
  .legend i { width: 11px; height: 11px; border-radius: 2px; display: inline-block; margin-right: 5px; vertical-align: -1px; }
  .fig-note { color: var(--slate); font-size: .74rem; line-height: 1.55; margin: 8px 0 0; }
  .fig-note b { color: var(--graphite); }
  .fig-note a { color: var(--slate); }
  .lnk { color: var(--atomdrift-blue); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 2px; white-space: nowrap; }

  /* ---------- frontier ---------- */
  .frontier-wrap { margin: 14px 0 4px; overflow-x: auto; }
  .frontier-wrap svg { width: 100%; min-width: 520px; height: auto; display: block; }
  .q-grid { stroke: var(--border); stroke-width: 1; }
  .q-axis { stroke: var(--slate); stroke-width: 1.25; }
  .q-div { stroke: var(--slate); stroke-width: 1; stroke-dasharray: 5 5; opacity: .45; }
  .q-tick { fill: var(--slate); font-size: 11px; font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; }
  .q-alab { fill: var(--iron); font-size: 12.5px; font-weight: 600; font-family: 'Oxanium', sans-serif; }
  .q-target { fill: var(--toxic); opacity: .11; }
  .q-quad { fill: var(--slate); opacity: .6; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .09em;
            text-transform: uppercase; font-family: 'Oxanium', sans-serif; }
  .q-quad.on-target { fill: var(--toxic-dark); opacity: .95; }
  .q-leader { stroke: var(--slate); stroke-width: 1; opacity: .45; }
  .q-pt { stroke: #fff; stroke-width: 2; }
  .q-count { fill: #fff; font-size: 11.5px; font-weight: 800; font-family: 'Oxanium', sans-serif; }
  .q-swatch { rx: 1.5; }
  .q-name { fill: var(--carbon); font-size: 12.5px; font-weight: 600; font-family: 'Oxanium', sans-serif; }
  .q-val { fill: var(--slate); font-size: 10.5px; font-weight: 500; font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; }
  /* Drawn first so it sits behind every dot and label, and dotted so it reads as
     'anywhere along here' rather than as another engine's series. */
  .q-curve { fill: none; stroke: #a8cbf0; stroke-width: 3; stroke-linecap: round;
             stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-dasharray: 1 7; }
  .q-cpt { stroke: #fff; stroke-width: 2.5; }
  .q-clab { font-family: 'SF Mono', Consolas, monospace; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; }
  .q-csub { fill: var(--slate); font-size: 10px; font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; }
  .q-cname { font-family: 'Oxanium', sans-serif; font-size: 12px; font-weight: 800; }

  /* ---------- rent vs own ---------- */
  .own { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 14px; margin-top: 6px; }
  .own .col { border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 10px; padding: 13px 16px; }
  .own .col.us { border-color: var(--toxic-dark); background: var(--toxic-dim); }
  .own .lbl { font-family: 'Oxanium', sans-serif; font-size: .68rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: .09em;
              text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--slate); display: block; margin-bottom: 9px; }
  .own .col.us .lbl { color: var(--toxic-dark); }
  .own ul { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 7px; }
  .own li { position: relative; padding-left: 19px; font-size: .78rem; line-height: 1.45; color: var(--graphite); }
  .own .them li::before { content: "✗"; position: absolute; left: 0; top: 0; color: #e5484d; font-weight: 800; font-size: .7rem; }
  .own .us li::before { content: "✓"; position: absolute; left: 0; top: 0; color: var(--toxic-dark); font-weight: 800; font-size: .7rem; }
  .own .us li { color: var(--carbon); }

  /* ---------- trend ---------- */
  .trend-wrap { position: relative; height: 340px; margin-top: 10px; }

  /* ---------- samples + method ---------- */
  /* Folded by default: it is the evidence, not the argument. Anyone who wants to
     check the work opens it; nobody else has to scroll past 43 filenames. */
  details.appendix { margin-top: 40px; border-top: 1px solid var(--border); padding-top: 6px; }
  details.appendix > summary { cursor: pointer; list-style: none; display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 9px;
    flex-wrap: wrap; font-family: 'Oxanium', sans-serif; font-weight: 800; font-size: 1.05rem; color: var(--carbon);
    padding: 8px 0; letter-spacing: -.3px; }
  details.appendix > summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  details.appendix > summary::before { content: "▸"; color: var(--toxic-dark); font-size: .8rem; transition: transform .15s ease; }
  details.appendix[open] > summary::before { transform: rotate(90deg); }
  details.appendix > summary:hover { color: var(--atomdrift-blue); }
  .samples { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 2px 22px; font-family: 'SF Mono', Consolas, monospace;
             font-size: .74rem; margin-top: 10px; }
  .samples a { color: var(--atomdrift-blue); text-decoration: none; white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden;
               text-overflow: ellipsis; display: block; }
  .samples a::before { content: "▪ "; color: var(--h); }
  .samples a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
  .meth { background: var(--cream); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 15px;
          font-size: .78rem; color: var(--graphite); line-height: 1.65; margin-top: 14px; }
  .meth b { color: var(--carbon); font-family: 'Oxanium', sans-serif; }
  .closer { margin-top: 34px; background: var(--carbon); border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px 24px; display: flex;
            justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; gap: 20px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .closer h4 { color: #fff; font-size: 1.05rem; letter-spacing: -.5px; margin: 0; font-family: 'Oxanium', sans-serif; font-weight: 800; }
  .closer p { margin: 5px 0 0; color: #b9bfc9; font-size: .78rem; }
  .closer .inst { font-family: 'SF Mono', Consolas, monospace; font-size: .78rem; background: #1c1f1a; color: var(--toxic);
                  border-radius: 7px; padding: 10px 15px; }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .cmp-hero { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 20px; }
    .own, .samples { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .cmp-hero h1 { font-size: 1.7rem; }
  }
</style>

{# One palette for every engine — hostile solid, suspicious pale, both anchored left;
   a grey tail on the right for files the engine could not open, which the methodology
   scores as a miss. Atomdrift is identified by its label, not by a colour of its own. #}
{%- macro rows(board) -%}
  <div class="rows">
  {%- for s in board | byFlagged %}
    {%- set p = providers[s.scanner] %}
    {%- set unsup = s.unsupported or 0 %}
    <div class="rn{% if s.scanner == 'ascan' %} us{% endif %}">{{ p.name or s.scanner }}</div>
    <div class="tr" title="{{ s.hostile }} hostile · {{ s.suspicious }} suspicious · {{ s.benign }} analyzed clean{% if s.nodata %} · {{ s.nodata }} no record{% endif %}{% if s.errored %} · {{ s.errored }} errored{% endif %}{% if unsup %} · {{ unsup }} couldn't open{% endif %}">
      {%- if s.supported > 0 -%}
        {%- if s.hostile > 0 %}<span class="hb" style="width:{{ s.hostile / s.supported * 100 }}%"></span>{% endif -%}
        {%- if s.suspicious > 0 %}<span class="sb" style="width:{{ s.suspicious / s.supported * 100 }}%"></span>{% endif -%}
        {%- if unsup > 0 %}<span class="un" style="width:{{ unsup / s.supported * 100 }}%"></span>{% endif -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
    {%- if s.supported %}
    <div class="rv">{{ (100 * (s.hostile + s.suspicious) / s.supported) | round(0) }}%</div>
    {%- else %}
    <div class="rv" style="color:var(--slate)">—</div>
    {%- endif %}
  {%- endfor %}
  </div>
{%- endmacro -%}

<div class="main-full compare">

  {%- set h = battle | headline %}
  {%- set curve = battle | ascanCurve %}

  <div class="cmp-hero">
    <div>
      <div class="kick">Atomdrift Scan · zero-day supply-chain detection · Apache-2.0</div>
      <h1>The open-source malware scanner that <em>doesn't suck</em>.</h1>
      <p class="say"><b>Probably the best malware scanner in the world</b> — an odd thing to say about something you
        can install in twenty seconds and read the source of. No cloud, no API key, no queue: it decides the first time
        it sees a file, which is the only way to catch a supply-chain attack on day zero.</p>
      {# The durable claim. A rival can out-detect us next week — detection leads are
         temporary. None of them can become something you embed, air-gap, or fork. #}
      <p class="replaces">Replaces <b>Socket</b>, <b>Aikido</b>, <b>ReversingLabs</b>. A rival might out-detect us
        next week — none of them can be air-gapped, embedded in a product you sell, or forked.</p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="cta cta-primary" href="/scan/">Install it →</a>
        <a class="cta" href="#how">How it decides</a>
        <a class="cta" href="#samples">See every sample</a>
      </div>
      <div class="instl">brew install atomdrift/tap/scan</div>
      <div class="badges">
        <span class="bdg hot">Apache-2.0</span>
        <span class="bdg">{{ providers.ascan.registries | length }} registries</span>
        <span class="bdg">95 file types</span>
        <span class="bdg">no telemetry</span>
        {# Longest badge last: it is ~3x the width of the others, so anything after it
           gets stranded alone on the next row. #}
        <span class="bdg">Linux · macOS · BSD · illumos</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    {%- if h %}
    <div class="proof">
      <div class="scores">
        <div><div class="n">{{ h.detRate }}%</div><div class="l">malware caught</div></div>
        {%- if h.fpRate != null %}
        <div><div class="n fp">{{ h.fpRate }}%</div><div class="l">false alarms</div></div>
        {%- endif %}
      </div>
      {%- if curve %}
      {# Server-rendered at the shipped default, so the numbers above are right with
         JS off; the slider only ever re-reads values already in the page. #}
      <div class="dial">
        <input type="range" id="dial" min="0" max="{{ (curve | length) - 1 }}"
               value="{{ curve | curveIndex(dialDefault) }}" step="1"
               aria-label="False-positive budget, -l 0 to -l {{ (curve | last).l }}">
        <div class="ends"><span>-l 0</span><span>-l {{ (curve | last).l }}</span></div>
      </div>
      {%- endif %}
      <div class="at">at <b id="dial-l">-l {{ dialDefault }}</b> <span id="dial-say">— the shipped default, untuned</span></div>
      <p><b>{{ battle.detection.sample_count }} live malware samples</b>, none older than 48 hours, against
        {{ battle.false_positive.sample_count }} known-good packages. Rebuilt daily; every sample published below.</p>
    </div>
    {%- endif %}
  </div>

  <h2 id="results">Latest results <span class="note">{{ battle.detection.sample_count }} malware samples, none older than 48 hours · {{ battle.generated_at | dateDisplay }}</span></h2>
  {{ rows(battle.detection.leaderboard) }}
  <div class="legend">
    <span><i style="background:var(--h)"></i>flagged hostile</span>
    <span><i style="background:var(--s)"></i>flagged suspicious</span>
    <span><i style="background:var(--na)"></i>couldn't open the file</span>
  </div>
  {%- if curve %}
  {%- set curveTop = curve | last %}
  {%- set curveDef = curve | curveStop(dialDefault) %}
  <p class="fig-note">Every engine is scored at its most sensitive setting, ours included.
    <b>{{ curveTop.det }}%</b> is the top of our dial; <b>{{ curveDef.det }}%</b> is the shipped default. Both beat
    the field.</p>
  <p class="fig-note">Grey is scope: engines that only read packages from registries they support can't open the rest
    of the cohort.</p>
  {%- else %}
  <p class="fig-note"><b>Grey is scope</b> — engines that only read packages from registries they support can't open the rest of the cohort.</p>
  {%- endif %}

  {%- set q = battle | quadrant(chartProviders) %}
  {%- if q %}
  <h2 id="frontier">Catch rate vs. false alarms <span class="note">everyone else is a dot — we're the line</span></h2>
  <p class="sub">Only the top-right corner counts: catch the malware, spare the clean code.
    <b>Every rival is one dot — one setting, chosen for you. We're the curve.</b></p>
  <div class="frontier-wrap">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 {{ q.w }} {{ q.h }}" role="img" aria-label="Catch rate against false-alarm rate for every engine measured this run. The horizontal axis is catch rate, higher to the right; the vertical axis is false-alarm rate, inverted so fewer false alarms is higher. Atomdrift is drawn as a curve across its false-positive budget settings rather than a single point. Per-engine figures are listed in the tables below.">
      <rect class="q-target" x="{{ q.xDiv }}" y="{{ q.pt }}" width="{{ q.pr - q.xDiv }}" height="{{ q.yDiv - q.pt }}"></rect>
      {%- for t in q.xTicks %}
      <line class="q-grid" x1="{{ t.x }}" y1="{{ q.pt }}" x2="{{ t.x }}" y2="{{ q.pb }}"></line>
      <text class="q-tick" x="{{ t.x }}" y="{{ q.pb + 19 }}" text-anchor="middle">{{ t.v }}%</text>
      {%- endfor %}
      {%- for t in q.yTicks %}
      <line class="q-grid" x1="{{ q.pl }}" y1="{{ t.y }}" x2="{{ q.pr }}" y2="{{ t.y }}"></line>
      <text class="q-tick" x="{{ q.pl - 10 }}" y="{{ t.y + 4 }}" text-anchor="end">{{ t.v }}%</text>
      {%- endfor %}
      <line class="q-div" x1="{{ q.xDiv }}" y1="{{ q.pt }}" x2="{{ q.xDiv }}" y2="{{ q.pb }}"></line>
      <line class="q-div" x1="{{ q.pl }}" y1="{{ q.yDiv }}" x2="{{ q.pr }}" y2="{{ q.yDiv }}"></line>
      <text class="q-quad" x="{{ q.pl + 10 }}" y="{{ q.pt + 20 }}">Conservative</text>
      <text class="q-quad on-target" x="{{ q.pr - 10 }}" y="{{ q.pt + 20 }}" text-anchor="end">The corner that matters</text>
      <text class="q-quad" x="{{ q.pl + 10 }}" y="{{ q.pb - 10 }}">Trailing</text>
      <text class="q-quad" x="{{ q.pr - 10 }}" y="{{ q.pb - 10 }}" text-anchor="end">Aggressive</text>
      <line class="q-axis" x1="{{ q.pl }}" y1="{{ q.pt }}" x2="{{ q.pl }}" y2="{{ q.pb }}"></line>
      <line class="q-axis" x1="{{ q.pl }}" y1="{{ q.pb }}" x2="{{ q.pr }}" y2="{{ q.pb }}"></line>
      <text class="q-alab" x="{{ (q.pl + q.pr) / 2 }}" y="{{ q.pb + 46 }}" text-anchor="middle">Malware caught  ·  higher is better →</text>
      <text class="q-alab" transform="translate(18,{{ (q.pt + q.pb) / 2 }}) rotate(-90)" text-anchor="middle">False alarms  ·  fewer is better ↑</text>

      {# Atomdrift's operating range, painted before anything else so no dot or
         label ever sits under it. Dotted because it is a range you choose a point
         on, not a series of measurements over time. #}
      {%- if q.curve %}<polyline class="q-curve" points="{{ q.curve.points }}"></polyline>{% endif %}

      {# competitors: one fixed point each #}
      {%- for g in q.groups %}
      {%- if g.leader %}<line class="q-leader" x1="{{ g.leader.x1 }}" y1="{{ g.leader.y1 }}" x2="{{ g.leader.x2 }}" y2="{{ g.leader.y2 }}"></line>{% endif %}
      <circle class="q-pt" cx="{{ g.x }}" cy="{{ g.y }}" r="{{ g.r }}" fill="{{ g.color }}">
        <title>{% for e in g.engines %}{{ e.name }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %} — {{ g.value }}</title>
      </circle>
      {%- if g.n > 1 %}<text class="q-count" x="{{ g.x }}" y="{{ g.y + 4 }}" text-anchor="middle">{{ g.n }}</text>{% endif %}
      {%- for e in g.engines %}
      {%- set ly = g.label.y + (loop.index0 * q.lineH) + 11 %}
      {%- if g.n > 1 %}
      {%- set sx = (g.label.tx - 9) if g.label.anchor == 'end' else (g.label.x if g.label.anchor == 'start' else g.label.tx - (e.name | length * 3.7) - 13) %}
      <rect class="q-swatch" x="{{ sx }}" y="{{ ly - 8 }}" width="8" height="8" fill="{{ e.color }}"></rect>
      {%- endif %}
      {%- set nx = (g.label.tx - 15 if g.n > 1 else g.label.tx) if g.label.anchor == 'end' else (g.label.x + 14 if g.n > 1 else g.label.x) %}
      <text class="q-name" x="{{ g.label.tx if g.label.anchor == 'middle' else nx }}" y="{{ ly }}" text-anchor="{{ g.label.anchor }}">{{ e.name }}</text>
      {%- endfor %}
      <text class="q-val" x="{{ g.label.tx }}" y="{{ g.label.y + (g.engines | length * q.lineH) + 11 }}" text-anchor="{{ g.label.anchor }}">{{ g.value }}</text>
      {%- endfor %}

      {%- if q.curve %}
      {%- for v in q.curve.verts %}
      <circle class="q-cpt" cx="{{ v.x }}" cy="{{ v.y }}" r="{{ 8 if v.label else 5 }}" fill="{{ q.curve.color }}">
        <title>atomscan -l {{ v.lLo }}{% if v.lHi != v.lLo %}–{{ v.lHi }}{% endif %} — {{ v.det }}% caught · {{ v.fp }}% false alarms</title>
      </circle>
      {%- if v.label %}
      <text class="q-clab" x="{{ v.x + 12 }}" y="{{ v.y - 2 }}" fill="{{ q.curve.color }}">{{ v.label }}</text>
      <text class="q-csub" x="{{ v.x + 12 }}" y="{{ v.y + 11 }}">{{ v.sub }}</text>
      {%- endif %}
      {%- endfor %}
      <text class="q-cname" x="{{ q.curve.verts[0].x - 12 }}" y="{{ q.curve.verts[0].y + 4 }}" text-anchor="end" fill="{{ q.curve.color }}">{{ q.curve.name }}</text>
      {%- endif %}
    </svg>
  </div>
  {%- if q.curve %}
  <p class="fig-note"><code>-l</code> is a false-positive budget — files flagged per <b>100 million</b>, calibrated
    per file type. Not “medium sensitivity”; a number you can plan a pipeline around.</p>
  {%- endif %}
  <p class="fig-note">{{ battle.detection.sample_count }} malware samples against
    {{ battle.false_positive.sample_count }} known-good packages. False-alarm axis inverted, so up and right is
    better.</p>
  {%- endif %}

  <h2 id="over-time">Every run before this one <span class="note">each draws a fresh cohort from live feeds</span></h2>
  <p class="sub">Most engines swing sixty points as the cohort changes. <b>Judge us on the worst night, not the
    best.</b></p>
  {%- if history and history | length >= 2 %}
  <div class="trend-wrap"><canvas id="trendChart"></canvas></div>
  <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/chart.js@4.4.1/dist/chart.umd.min.js"></script>
  <script>
  (function () {
    // Truncate to the 2026-07-09 10:00 UTC pipeline overhaul: earlier runs measured a
    // materially different detection stack, so plotting them would misrepresent the
    // current engines. Non-destructive — history.json keeps every point.
    const CUTOFF = Date.parse('2026-07-09T10:00:00Z');
    const H = ({{ history | dump | safe }}).filter(p => Date.parse(p.at) >= CUTOFF);
    const PROV = {{ chartProviders | dump | safe }};
    // Engine colors and draw order come from providers.json — one declaration
    // shared with the quadrant, so an engine keeps its hue across the page and
    // no engine can be missing from this chart just by being absent from a map
    // hardcoded here.
    const ENGINES = Object.keys(PROV).filter(e => !PROV[e].hidden)
      .sort((a, b) => (PROV[a].slot || 99) - (PROV[b].slot || 99));
    const COLOR = Object.fromEntries(ENGINES.map(e => [e, PROV[e].color || '#6b7280']));
    const US = 'ascan';
    const labels = H.map(p => new Date(p.at).toLocaleString(undefined, { month: 'short', day: 'numeric', hour: 'numeric' }));
    // Atomdrift is the subject: drawn last (on top) and heavier than the field.
    // The emphasis is weight only — fading the others toward the surface used to
    // collapse them into each other (seven lines at 45% alpha sit ~7 ΔE apart,
    // under the ~15 a full-color reader needs), so every line keeps its own hue.
    const order = ENGINES.filter(e => e !== US).concat(US);
    const datasets = order.map(e => {
      const us = e === US;
      return {
        label: (PROV[e] && (PROV[e].chartName || PROV[e].name)) || e,
        data: H.map(p => (p.detection && p.detection[e] != null) ? Math.round(p.detection[e] * 100) : null),
        solidColor: COLOR[e],
        borderColor: COLOR[e],
        backgroundColor: COLOR[e],
        borderWidth: us ? 4 : 2,
        pointRadius: us ? 4 : 2.5,
        pointHoverRadius: us ? 6 : 4,
        tension: 0.25, spanGaps: true
      };
    }).filter(d => d.data.some(v => v != null));

    // Each line's latest value, labelled at its right end so current standings read
    // without hovering; labels are nudged apart vertically when they'd collide.
    const endLabels = {
      id: 'endLabels',
      afterDatasetsDraw(chart) {
        const ctx = chart.ctx;
        const items = [];
        chart.data.datasets.forEach((ds, i) => {
          const meta = chart.getDatasetMeta(i);
          if (meta.hidden || !meta.data.length) return;
          for (let j = ds.data.length - 1; j >= 0; j--) {
            if (ds.data[j] != null) {
              items.push({ x: meta.data[j].x, y: meta.data[j].y, val: ds.data[j], color: ds.solidColor, bold: ds.borderWidth >= 3 });
              break;
            }
          }
        });
        items.sort((a, b) => a.y - b.y);
        for (let i = 1; i < items.length; i++) {
          if (items[i].y - items[i - 1].y < 13) items[i].y = items[i - 1].y + 13;
        }
        ctx.save();
        ctx.textBaseline = 'middle';
        items.forEach(it => {
          ctx.font = (it.bold ? '700 ' : '600 ') + '11px system-ui, sans-serif';
          ctx.fillStyle = it.color;
          ctx.fillText(it.val + '%', it.x + 7, it.y);
        });
        ctx.restore();
      }
    };

    new Chart(document.getElementById('trendChart'), {
      type: 'line',
      data: { labels, datasets },
      plugins: [endLabels],
      options: {
        responsive: true, maintainAspectRatio: false,
        interaction: { mode: 'index', intersect: false },
        layout: { padding: { right: 46, left: 6 } },
        scales: {
          y: { beginAtZero: true, max: 100, ticks: { callback: v => v + '%' }, title: { display: true, text: 'Catch rate' } }
        },
        plugins: {
          // Chart.js defaults to a 40px line-style box per entry, which pushes
          // eight engines onto three rows and eats the chart's height. Small
          // circular swatches keep the whole field on one or two rows.
          legend: {
            position: 'bottom',
            labels: {
              usePointStyle: true, pointStyle: 'circle', boxWidth: 8, boxHeight: 8, padding: 12, font: { size: 11 },
              // VirusTotal's engine count earns its width on a full-size chart, but
              // the eight entries only just fit one row — adding "[66 engines]" tips
              // them onto a second row once the canvas drops below ~730px, which
              // comes straight out of the plot's height. Under that, fall back to the
              // bare name: the count is still on the quadrant and the bars. Chart.js
              // regenerates labels on resize, so this re-evaluates as the page reflows.
              generateLabels(chart) {
                const items = Chart.defaults.plugins.legend.labels.generateLabels(chart);
                if (chart.width < 730) {
                  for (const it of items) it.text = it.text.replace(/ \[\d+ engines\]$/, '');
                }
                return items;
              }
            }
          },
          tooltip: {
            itemSort: (a, b) => b.parsed.y - a.parsed.y,
            callbacks: { label: c => `${c.dataset.label}: ${c.parsed.y}%` }
          }
        }
      }
    });
  })();
  </script>
  <p class="fig-note">Each run draws a fresh cohort, so movement is the samples changing as much as the engines.</p>
  {%- else %}
  <div class="meth">Historical trend is collecting. Each recorded run appends a snapshot; the chart appears once there
    are at least two.</div>
  {%- endif %}

  <h2 id="how">Why it wins <span class="note">everyone else looks it up — this one works it out</span></h2>
  <p class="sub">A hosted scanner asks a database what somebody else concluded. Nobody has looked at a day-zero
    package, so the answer is <em>no record</em> — and your gate reads that as <em>clean</em>. <b>Atomdrift asks
    nobody.</b> It disassembles the file, names what the code can <em>do</em> against 85,000 capability rules, and
    scores the combination locally. <a class="lnk" href="/scan/how-it-works/">How it works →</a></p>

  <h2 id="yours">It's yours <span class="note">Apache-2.0 — embed it, fork it, ship it in something you sell</span></h2>
  <p class="sub">Socket, Aikido, SafeDep and VirusTotal are services you rent. That's not a licensing detail — it
    decides what you're allowed to build.</p>
  <div class="own">
    <div class="col them">
      <span class="lbl">A scanner you rent</span>
      <ul>
        <li>Priced per seat or per scan, forever</li>
        <li>Needs the network — no air-gap, no offline CI</li>
        <li>Can't go inside a product you sell</li>
        <li>Can't see the rule that flagged your build</li>
        <li>A false positive gets fixed when the vendor feels like it</li>
        <li>Your code goes to their servers</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="col us">
      <span class="lbl">A scanner you own</span>
      <ul>
        <li>Apache-2.0. Free at any volume, forever</li>
        <li>One static binary. Runs air-gapped, on five operating systems</li>
        <li>Embed it in your own product and sell that</li>
        <li>Every rule is a YAML file in a public repo</li>
        <li>Fix it yourself this afternoon, or fork it</li>
        <li>Nothing leaves your network. Ever</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="sub" style="margin-top:14px"><b>5,027,887 rules</b> ship with it, and <b>every one is a YAML file in a
    public repo</b> — nobody else lets you read the rule that flagged your build, let alone change it. It runs four
    ways, all returning the same JSON, so the integration you write once scales from a git hook to a registry.</p>
  </div>

  {%- set badSamples = battle.samples | where("label", "bad") -%}
  {%- set cohortBad = badSamples | whereUnset("excluded") -%}
  {%- if curve %}
  <script>
  (function () {
    // Every point is a measurement from this run — atomscan's lvl is swept across the
    // whole grid on each scan, so one pass describes the cohort at every deploy level.
    const C = {{ curve | dump | safe }}, DEF = {{ dialDefault }};
    const el = document.getElementById('dial');
    if (!el || !C.length) return;
    const det = document.querySelector('.proof .n'), fp = document.querySelector('.proof .n.fp');
    const lab = document.getElementById('dial-l'), say = document.getElementById('dial-say');
    const stance = (l) => l === DEF ? '— the shipped default, untuned'
      : (l === 0 ? '— zero false-positive budget' : (l < DEF ? '— stricter than the default' : '— tuned for recall'));
    function render() {
      const c = C[+el.value];
      if (det) det.textContent = c.det + '%';
      if (fp) fp.textContent = c.fp + '%';
      lab.textContent = '-l ' + c.l.toLocaleString('en-US');
      say.textContent = stance(c.l);
    }
    el.addEventListener('input', render);
    render();
  })();
  </script>
  {%- endif %}

  <details class="appendix" id="samples">
    <summary>Appendix — samples and methodology <span class="note">all {{ cohortBad | length }}, every one linked</span></summary>
  <div class="samples">
    {%- for sm in cohortBad | sort(false, false, "filename") %}
    <a href="https://lab.atomdrift.org/file/{{ sm.sha256 }}" title="{{ sm.filename }}{% if sm.ecosystem %} · {{ sm.ecosystem }}{% endif %} — view in Atomdrift Lab">{{ sm.purl or sm.filename }}</a>
    {%- endfor %}
  </div>
  <div class="meth"><b>How this is scored.</b> Samples come from live public malware feeds, none older than 48 hours.
    Every engine gets the identical cohort, and a skip counts as a miss for all of them — a file nobody scanned is a
    file that got through. A listing from a feed belonging to one of the contestants only counts once an independent
    engine corroborates it, so nobody is credited for their own report. Presumed-good packages are drawn from the
    freshest 48 hours of the open-source firehose and re-checked on later runs: one that turns out to be malware is
    removed from that run's false-positive rates, and any engine that flagged it is credited with an early detection.
    <b>We run this benchmark and we're one of the engines in it</b>, so every sample, verdict and rate is published.
    {%- if battle.versions %}
    <br><br><b>Engine versions:</b> {% for k, v in battle.versions %}{% if not loop.first %} · {% endif %}{{ providers[k].name or k }} {{ v }}{% endfor %}
    <span style="color:var(--slate)">(locally-run engines; {% for p in providers | bySlot | selectattr("hosted") %}{{ p.name }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %} are hosted services queried live, so they carry no pinned version).</span>
    {%- endif %}
  </div>
  </details>

  <div class="closer">
    <div>
      <h4>Point it at your dependencies.</h4>
      <p>Apache-2.0 · runs offline · twenty seconds to first scan</p>
    </div>
    <div class="inst">brew install atomdrift/tap/scan</div>
  </div>

</div>
